{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nAlice and Bob have decided to play the game \"Rock, Paper, Scissors\". The game consists of several rounds, each round is independent of each other. In each round, both players show one of the following things at the same time: rock, paper or scissors. If both players showed the same things then the round outcome is a draw. Otherwise, the following rules applied:  if one player showed rock and the other one showed scissors, then the player who showed rock is considered the winner and the other one is considered the loser;  if one player showed scissors and the other one showed paper, then the player who showed scissors is considered the winner and the other one is considered the loser;  if one player showed paper and the other one showed rock, then the player who showed paper is considered the winner and the other one is considered the loser. Alice and Bob decided to play exactly $$$n$$$ rounds of the game described above. Alice decided to show rock $$$a_1$$$ times, show scissors $$$a_2$$$ times and show paper $$$a_3$$$ times. Bob decided to show rock $$$b_1$$$ times, show scissors $$$b_2$$$ times and show paper $$$b_3$$$ times. Though, both Alice and Bob did not choose the sequence in which they show things. It is guaranteed that $$$a_1 + a_2 + a_3 = n$$$ and $$$b_1 + b_2 + b_3 = n$$$.Your task is to find two numbers:  the minimum number of round Alice can win;  the maximum number of rounds Alice can win. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Python 3 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nn=int(input())\na=list(map(int,input().rstrip().split()))\nb=list(map(int,input().rstrip().split()))\na1,a2,a3=a\nb1,b2,b3=b\nans=[0,0]\nans[1]=min(a1,b2)+min(a2,b3)+min(a3,b1)\nans[0]=max(0,b1-(a1+a2),b2-(a2+a3),b3-(a1+a3))\nprint(*ans)\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nn = int(input())\na = list(map(int, input().rstrip().split()))\nb = list(map(int, input().rstrip().split()))\na1, a2, a3 = a\nb1, b2, b3 = b\n\n# Maximum wins for Alice:\n# Alice wins when:\n#   rock (a1) beats scissors (b2)\n#   scissors (a2) beats paper (b3)\n#   paper (a3) beats rock (b1)\nmax_wins = min(a1, b2) + min(a2, b3) + min(a3, b1)\n\n# Minimum wins for Alice:\n# We want to minimize Alice's wins, which means maximizing draws and Bob's wins.\n# Total rounds = n\n# Alice's wins + Bob's wins + draws = n\n# So minimum Alice wins = n - (maximum possible draws + maximum possible Bob wins)\n\n# For Bob's wins:\n# Bob wins when:\n#   rock (b1) beats scissors (a2)\n#   scissors (b2) beats paper (a3)\n#   paper (b3) beats rock (a1)\nbob_wins = min(b1, a2) + min(b2, a3) + min(b3, a1)\n\n# Draws occur when both show the same thing:\ndraws = min(a1, b1) + min(a2, b2) + min(a3, b3)\n\n# But we need to be careful - the maximum total of draws + bob_wins might exceed n\n# Actually, the minimum wins for Alice is: n - (maximum possible non-wins for Alice)\n# Non-wins for Alice = draws + bob_wins, but we need to compute the maximum possible non-wins\n\n# Alternative approach: use the formula for minimum wins\n# Minimum wins = max(0, a1 - b1 - b3, a2 - b2 - b1, a3 - b3 - b2)\n# This is because:\n# - Alice's rock (a1) can only win against Bob's scissors (b2)\n#   So to minimize Alice's wins with rock, we want to use her rock against Bob's rock (b1) and paper (b3) as much as possible\n#   Maximum non-win for Alice's rock = min(a1, b1 + b3)\n#   So minimum wins from Alice's rock = max(0, a1 - (b1 + b3))\n# Similarly for other moves\n\nmin_wins = max(0, a1 - b1 - b3, a2 - b2 - b1, a3 - b3 - b2)\n\nprint(min_wins, max_wins)\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.95\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-4.768370445162873e-07, -3.2305197237292305e-05, -2.7418097943154862e-06, -1.0728830375228426e-06, -0.0017263285117223859, -0.2811655104160309, -1.5497195136049413e-06, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -0.0017062166007235646, -2.264974000354414e-06, -0.0004967409186065197, -3.2186455882765586e-06, -5.960462772236497e-07, -8.106198947643861e-06, -1.8954096958623268e-05, -7.271740287251305e-06, -3.576278118089249e-07, -0.005497219506651163, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -3.099436753473128e-06, -5.602820692729438e-06, -3.6000557884108275e-05, -6.6756979322235566e-06, -3.814689989667386e-06, -2.13382354559144e-05, -1.4424220353248529e-05, -5.960462772236497e-07, -1.7881377516459906e-06, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -6.318072337307967e-06, -1.311301275563892e-06, -2.7418097943154862e-06, -0.0005559567362070084, -4.768370445162873e-07, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -1.5258672647178173e-05, 0.0, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -9.536738616588991e-07, -4.768370445162873e-07, -3.576278118089249e-07, 0.0, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -2.264974000354414e-06, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -3.3378546504536644e-06, -7.152555099310121e-07, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -7.986990567587782e-06, -4.0531076592742465e-06, -9.536738616588991e-07, -3.2186455882765586e-06, -0.005930209998041391, -6.3774932641536e-05, -0.05148617550730705, -0.011225